## Chapter 4: Building the Base
Three weeks passed in the grey, suffocating rhythm of Anvil Ridge Vocational.
To the rest of the world, Kaelen Vance was the ideal bottom-tier student—quiet, exhausted, and utterly unremarkable. He sat in the back of Instructor Horgan’s lectures on basic sanitation protocols, took diligent notes on how to properly sort industrial scrap, and scored perfectly in the middle of his physical conditioning evaluations.
During the day, he wore his uniform jacket slightly loose to hide the subtle change in his posture. When Horgan made the class do three-mile runs around the cracked asphalt yard, Kaelen purposely dropped toward the back of the pack, breathing heavily and wiping sweat from his forehead as if he were on the verge of collapse.
He played the part of a struggling, weak Tier-E Salvager to absolute perfection.
"Look at Vance," a Tier-D *Sentry* student chuckled one afternoon while leaning against the barracks wall, watching Kaelen lug a heavy box of rusted gears toward the sorting sheds. "Guy’s been taking every low-paying sewer job on the board just to buy extra nutrient packets. He's going to blow his back out before we even reach mid-terms."
Kaelen kept his head down, clutching the wooden crate, and let out a soft, strained grunt for effect.
What the student didn't see—what no one was allowed to see—was that under Kaelen’s worn uniform, every muscle fibre was dense, honed, and rewired. The heavy wooden box felt like a shoe carton in his hands.
Every night, while his roommates slept or snored off their fatigue, Kaelen snuck out through the broken security fence behind the West Barracks. He slipped into the dark, labyrinthine alleys of Sector 9, taking on the dirtiest, most dangerous odd jobs that the Auxiliary Guild board had to offer.
The sewers. The abandoned chemical plants. The collapsed underground train tunnels.
He didn't care about the pitiful credit payouts. He cared about the corpses left behind by the high-tier Hunter guilds, who only harvested the primary cores and left the meat, bone, and secondary traits to rot.
In an abandoned subway maintenance tunnel beneath the industrial district, the air was thick with the stench of stagnant water and ozone.
Kaelen stood over the body of a **Grave-Crawler**—a massive, six-legged subterranean creature with a shell made of hardened chiton and eyes that blazed with a faint, ghostly blue light. Its skull was shattered where Kaelen had driven a reinforced steel pipe straight through its eye socket using precise, ruthless leverage.
Kaelen wiped a smudge of black blood from his jaw and held his bare palm over the creature's cracked thorax.
"**[Deconstruct]**," he murmured.
The dark, blood-red glitch text flickered instantly in his field of vision as gray vapor swirled out from his skin, enveloping the six-foot beast. Within seconds, the thick armor plating withered into fine grey dust, leaving behind a glowing, deep-blue crystal fragment floating in the dark.
**[Unmapped Architectural Parameter Triggered.]**
**[Deconstructing Aetheric Fragment: Filtering Grave-Crawler Corruption...]**
**[Purification Complete.]**
**[Absorb Purified Trait Shard: Sub-Dermal Density (Minor)?]**
"Absorb," Kaelen commanded quietly.
The blue crystal dissolved, sinking straight into his pores.
Instantly, a brutal, crushing sensation washed over his chest and arms. It felt as though his ribcage were being squeezed in a hydraulic press, his bone marrow expanding and hardening to absorb the mineral traits of the subterranean beast. His teeth clicked together violently as he gritted through the sharp, white-hot ache.
He didn't make a sound. He had consumed over twenty shards in three weeks; he was used to the agony. He knew that the pain was simply his biology reorganizing itself into something far more dangerous.
After forty seconds, the pressure subsided into a solid, heavy warmth resting deep beneath his skin.
Kaelen opened his eyes, letting out a slow, steady exhale of hot air. He summoned his status screen.
**[Kaelen Vance]**
 * **Class:** Salvager (Tier-E)
 * **Level:** 1
 * **Strength:** **18 (+10)** | **Agility:** **21 (+12)** | **Vitality:** **19 (+11)** | **Aetheric Resonance:** **4 (+2)**
 * **Active Skills:**
   * **[Deconstruct (Lv.2)]** - *Efficiency: 55%. Target expanded to Organic & Crystallized Essence.*
 * **Passive Traits Assimilated:**
   * **[Dense Fiber Network (Tier-E)]** - *Sub-dermal muscle density increased by 25%. Minor resistance to blunt impacts.*
   * **[Low-Light Spatial Perception (Tier-E)]** - *Visual acuity enhanced in subterranean/dark environments.*
Kaelen stared at the numbers.
A standard Tier-E auxiliary student at Level 1 had stats that hovered around 8 or 9 across the board. A high-tier student—a Tier-B *Knight* or Tier-A *Spellblade* from an elite academy like Ironclad—started with baseline stats around 20 to 25.
Without gaining a single class level, Kaelen had silently bridged the gap between a garbage man and an elite academy prodigy simply by eating the scrap they left behind.
He closed his fist, feeling the tremendous, latent kinetic force packed into his forearm. If he hit a standard concrete wall right now, his knuckles wouldn't break; the wall would.
*Step. Step. Step.*
Kaelen’s eyes snapped toward the dark end of the subway tunnel. His **[Low-Light Spatial Perception]** flared instantly, painting the shadow-drenched tunnel in sharp shades of grey and faint blue outlines.
A military patrol drone—a small, hovering sphere equipped with a sweeping red scanner—was drifting down the maintenance shaft, checking for unauthorized breaches.
Kaelen didn't panic. He smoothly dropped his shoulders, let his posture slump, and picked up a rusted iron bucket filled with worthless copper wire he had collected earlier.
When the red scanning light washed over him, it pinged against his student badge.
"**Identification Verified: Kaelen Vance. Student ID: AV-8809.**" The drone's synthesized voice echoed off the damp walls. "**Authorized Auxiliary Salvage Assignment #089. Status: Compliant. Proceed with caution.**"
The drone lingered for a second, its optical sensor whirring as it recorded his faded uniform, dirty hands, and the bucket of scrap wire. Finding nothing out of the ordinary, it turned and drifted back up the shaft.
Kaelen watched it disappear into the darkness, his face completely emotionless.
"Keep scanning," he muttered under his breath, adjusting his grip on the rusted bucket. "Keep seeing what you expect to see."
He turned and began the long walk back toward the academy fence before dawn broke. The foundation was set. His body was transforming into a hidden fortress, locked behind a Tier-E mask that no one in this city would ever think to look past.
